  <p>One note up front: price and value are not the same thing here. The cheapest scan is often the most expensive outcome, because an inaccurate or incomplete existing-conditions dataset surfaces as a change order during construction, when fixing it costs ten times what the scan did. Scope the work correctly and the number below is some of the cheapest insurance on your project.</p>

  <h2>The five things that drive the price</h2>

  <p><strong>1. Square footage.</strong> The single biggest factor. On-site capture time and processing both scale with size, though not perfectly linearly, a 20,000 SF building does not cost twice a 10,000 SF one, because setup and travel are fixed costs spread across more area. Larger projects almost always get a better per-square-foot rate.</p>

  <p><strong>2. Scope of capture.</strong> Interior only is cheaper than interior plus exterior plus roof plus site. Every added surface is more time on site and more to process. Be explicit about what you need, a quote that assumes "everything" is a quote for everything.</p>

  <p><strong>3. The deliverable.</strong> This is where costs diverge the most. A raw point cloud is inexpensive because the scanner does the work. 2D CAD drawings and especially a Revit BIM model require skilled human hours to produce, and the level of detail (<a href="/resources/ultimate-guide-as-built-drawings/">LOD</a>) multiplies those hours. MEP modeling is the single largest cost adder.</p>

  <p><strong>4. Accuracy level.</strong> The <a href="/resources/ultimate-guide-as-built-drawings/">USIBD Level of Accuracy</a> you specify changes how carefully control is established and how densely the space is captured. LOA 30 for a renovation is routine; LOA 40 or 50 for prefabrication or historic work means more setups, tighter control, and more QA, which costs more. Asking for LOA 50 on a job that only needs LOA 30 can roughly double your bill for accuracy you'll never use.</p>

  <p><strong>5. Access and logistics.</strong> An occupied building scanned after hours, a secured facility requiring badging, high ceilings needing a lift, or a remote site with travel and per-diem all add cost. These are usually the line items people forget to mention when they ask for a quote, and then the quote changes.</p>



