  <h2>Typical ranges by deliverable</h2>

  <p>For a notional mid-size commercial building, order-of-magnitude figures look roughly like this. Treat them as a sanity check, not a quote:</p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Deliverable</th><th>Order of magnitude</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Raw point cloud only (archival record)</td><td>$400&ndash;$1,600</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2D as-built set (plans + RCP + elevations)</td><td>$3,000&ndash;$12,000</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Scan-to-BIM, LOD 200 (architectural)</td><td>$3,000&ndash;$5,000</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Scan-to-BIM, LOD 300 (coordinatable)</td><td>$5,000&ndash;$9,000</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Scan-to-BIM, LOD 400 (detailed MEP)</td><td>$9,000&ndash;$15,000+, often higher on MEP-dense sites</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>Two things worth noticing. First, the point cloud itself is cheap relative to the modeling, so if you're already paying for a scan, keeping the raw cloud as an archival deliverable costs almost nothing and protects you later. Second, small jobs carry a floor: a 400 SF suite and a 4,000 SF suite can cost nearly the same once you account for mobilization, so very small projects feel expensive per square foot.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <div class="callout-title">The most common way people overpay</div>
    <p>Ordering a detailed BIM model when 2D plans would have done the job. A kitchen remodel or a lease exhibit does not need an LOD 400 Revit model. The reverse mistake is cheaper up front but worse: ordering 2D plans for a project that later needs clash detection, and paying to re-scope. Match the deliverable to the actual use, and if you're not sure, ask the provider to recommend the minimum that works.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>How to control the cost</h2>

  <p><strong>Scope precisely.</strong> Send square footage, which surfaces you need captured, the deliverable format and version, the accuracy level, and the access constraints. A provider who can quote cleanly from that has scoped jobs like yours before. Our <a href="/resources/as-built-request-checklist/">request checklist</a> covers exactly what to send.</p>

  <p><strong>Scan once, deliver in phases.</strong> The expensive part is mobilizing and capturing. Scan the whole building once, take the point cloud now, and derive 2D or BIM later only for the areas and disciplines you actually need. This avoids paying for modeling you may never use while keeping the option open.</p>

  <p><strong>Bundle work.</strong> Multi-day and multi-site projects earn better rates because fixed costs spread further. If you have several buildings or a phased program, say so up front.</p>

  <p><strong>Keep the raw data.</strong> Insist on a vendor-neutral <code>.e57</code> point cloud alongside any proprietary format. If a new question comes up later, you answer it from the file instead of paying for a return visit.</p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>

  <p>3D laser scanning is priced on square footage, scope, deliverable, accuracy, and access, in that rough order of impact. A raw point cloud is a few hundred to a couple thousand dollars; a coordinatable Revit model of a mid-size building typically runs in the mid four to low five figures. The way to get a fair price is to scope the work precisely and order the minimum deliverable your project actually needs. Send us an address and a scope and you'll have a written quote within one business day, no "call for pricing" required.</p>
